Well, I know you guys are going to get tired of me asking questions, but I
haven't been able to find a good documentation source for setting up linux
RH 5.1 on this multia properly. First, this is a 166 mhz multia with built
in scsi II and external scsi II connector, 32m ram and of course the lousy
TGA 8 plane color - more about that later...

I am able to use the four floppys to load milo and then a kernel to install
linux (i assume) from the SRM. I am not using the Microsoft ARC console
although it is in the flash rom as well.

Here is the problem.

I am fine until disk partitioning time.

I am reasonably familiar with Unix as I have several other unix boxes in
this office - namely solaris sun sparcstations and one SGI running Irix.  I
know that in order for this hard drive that I pick to boot linux's milo, it
must be installed to a little tiny dos partition on the hard drive and I
also know from experience that I need a medium small partition +- 100M for
the / mountpoint and the rest can be allocated to /usr as the install
program should figure out and create any additional mountpoints I need...
The problem is that I can create the following partition map til I am blue
in the face, but when Linux starts it's install, it overwrites all that junk
and puts whatever it wants on the hard drive as far as partitions are
concerned, which overwrites my dos-16 partition so I cannot load milo and
linload.exe onto a little tiny partition - its no longer there and boot from
the hard drive. Now if I load milo off a floppy then boot the vmlinux.gz
partition from the / partition, it will go ahead and boot ok.

Here is my partition map as I want it

partition       size     type                    mount
1                 5M       DOS-16>32M
2               100M      Linux Swap
3                100M     Linux Native          /
4               850M      Linux Native          /usr


But instead, the linux install program does this:

sda1         870M         linux-native       /usr
sda5         307M         linux-native       /
sda6         101M         linux-swap

There are a few other problems minor but I will get to them when I can get
this darn thing to boot itself from SRM without a hassle.





